<%# Per-participant actions — VIEWER-SCOPED. Takes an explicit `viewer:` local
    (NOT the implicit current_user, which is undefined in a broadcast render context;
    using it would blank the opponent's buttons). accept!/conclude! broadcast this
    per-participant on each user-signed stream. Locals: debate, viewer (defaults to
    current_user in the request context). Pinned dom_id so the actions Turbo-Stream
    replaces land here; the root element always renders so a later replace has a target.

    DESIGN SYSTEM (Slice 9, Task 4.5) — all four controls take `ds_button_classes`. They
    were already the house pill (2px border, white fill, coloured text, `shadow`) at
    `px-4 py-1`, which is exactly the helper's `size: :sm`; what they were missing is the
    `text-sm` that size carries and the `active:scale-95` press. That is the "two views
    currently use `px-4 py-1` with no `text-sm`" drift `DesignSystemHelper` names in its
    own comments — this is one of them.

    "Waiting for @user to accept…" and "Maximum rounds reached" stay hand-spelled: the
    first is 14px muted body copy and the second is a 12px uppercase `tracking-wide`
    micro-label, the same treatment as the state label next to it. Neither is a button
    and there is no primitive for either. %>
<% viewer = local_assigns.fetch(:viewer) { current_user } %>
<span id="<%= dom_id(debate, :actions) %>" class="debate-actions flex items-center gap-2 shrink-0">
  <% if viewer.present? %>
    <% if debate.pending? && viewer == debate.opponent %>
      <%= button_to "Accept", accept_debate_path(debate.slug), method: :patch,
            class: ds_button_classes(variant: :outline, tone: "primary", size: :sm) %>
      <%= button_to "Decline", decline_debate_path(debate.slug), method: :patch,
            class: ds_button_classes(variant: :outline, tone: "grey", size: :sm) %>
    <% elsif debate.pending? && viewer == debate.challenger %>
      <span class="text-sm text-grey">Waiting for @<%= debate.opponent.username %> to accept…</span>
    <% elsif debate.active? && debate.participant?(viewer) %>
      <%# The extend window is the closing-round BOUNDARY only, so this button appears
          for exactly one moment in a debate's life. at_round_ceiling? is the second
          reason extendable_by? can be false, and the note below is why the model
          exposes it separately: a participant who has been extending round by round
          arrives at the boundary expecting the button they clicked last time, and
          silence there reads as a bug. Because rounds_limit only reaches MAX_ROUNDS
          BY extending (nothing else writes it), the ceiling is always reached at a
          boundary — so the note replaces the button in place rather than sitting on
          the page for the whole debate. %>
      <% if debate.extendable_by?(viewer) %>
        <%= button_to "Extend by one round", extend_debate_path(debate.slug),
              method: :post, class: ds_button_classes(variant: :outline, size: :sm) %>
      <% elsif debate.at_round_ceiling? %>
        <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-grey">Maximum rounds reached</span>
      <% end %>
      <%= button_to "Conclude", conclude_debate_path(debate.slug), method: :patch,
            class: ds_button_classes(variant: :outline, tone: "grey", size: :sm) %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</span>
